# nordtal.eu season 2 - deployment settings.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   $EDITOR .env               # replace every REPLACE_ME
#   docker compose up -d
#
# .env holds the Discord token, the bunq API key, the database password and the forwarding
# secret. It is gitignored and must never be committed. This repository is public.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# HOW TO READ THIS FILE
# ==============================================================================================
# REPLACE_ME   A value only you can supply. It is deliberately not a plausible-looking default:
#              every id below is validated, and REPLACE_ME fails that check by name -
#              "roles.access must be a Discord snowflake (digits only), was: REPLACE_ME" -
#              instead of starting a bot that writes into a channel nobody meant.
#
#              So a half-filled .env stops the stack with a message naming the one setting that
#              is still wrong. That is the whole design; do not replace REPLACE_ME with zeros to
#              "get past" it, because a row of zeros IS a valid snowflake and would start.
#
# Settings that are more than a single value - the language list, the price list - are written
# out in full below, filled with REPLACE_ME. Edit them in place: the shape is the documentation.
#
# Anything commented out is optional and already has a working default in the config file that
# owns it. Uncomment only to change it. An empty value means "not set" and is the same as
# leaving the line commented, so `FOO=` never means "empty string".
#
# The Discord ids are snowflakes. Discord: Settings -> Advanced -> Developer Mode on, then
# right-click a role or channel -> Copy Role ID / Copy Channel ID.

# ==============================================================================================
# What runs
# ==============================================================================================
# db  = PostgreSQL      bot = the Discord bot      mc = proxy + the three Paper servers
# backup = the pg_dump sidecar. Its own profile rather than part of `db`, because it is the one
# service that still makes sense against a database this compose does not own - and the one you
# must not lose silently by dropping `db`.
#
# `updater` is missing from this line because it HAS no profile: it is in every selection, always,
# including COMPOSE_PROFILES=bot. It is the only process that applies the schema and the only one
# that answers /update in Discord, so nothing else here can correctly run without it.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=db,bot,mc,backup

TZ=Europe/Berlin

# ==============================================================================================
# Versions
# ==============================================================================================
# THESE TWO ARE FOR BUILDING ON THIS MACHINE, AND A PRODUCTION HOST CAN IGNORE THEM ENTIRELY.
# Compose pulls every image from ghcr.io/nordtal and builds nothing, so all these two do is name the
# jar a local `docker compose build` copies into the bot's and the updater's images. Which release
# runs is IMAGE_TAG, further down.
#
# They are also not the version of anything that runs. Every jar in this deployment - the four
# servers' plugins, the bot's jar and the updater's own - is the jar lying in a volume, and the
# updater is what puts it there. `docker compose run --rm updater report` prints what is actually
# installed, and both containers print the jar they picked on every start.
SEASON_VERSION=0.2.3
# Must match gradle.properties for a local build to find the jar.
BOT_VERSION=0.2.3
# Where our own jars and the resource pack come from. Read by the updater.
SEASON_REPO=nordtal/season-2

# SEASON_RELEASE, PACK_URL and PACK_SHA1 are deliberately absent and must not come back.
#
#   - The updater owns the jars, and the release it FOLLOWS is UPDATER_SEASON_RELEASE below. Two
#     variables describing the same thing is one of them silently wrong.
#   - A jcore environment override wins over the file and is never written back, so the updater
#     could not write a new hash into pack.yml while PACK_URL and PACK_SHA1 were set. pack.yml in
#     the network-control volume is the place, and `updater bootstrap` writes it.

# ==============================================================================================
# The updater
# ==============================================================================================
# It runs all the time, and it never updates anything on its own. `serve` applies the schema at
# startup and then does nothing at all until somebody asks - from /update in Discord, from
# /smp update in game, or from one of these:
#
#   docker compose run --rm updater report   resolve and report, changes nothing
#   docker compose run --rm updater migrate  apply the database schema, nothing else
#   docker compose run --rm updater bootstrap    migrate, then fetch and place the files
#
# IT IS THE BOOTSTRAP OF THIS DEPLOYMENT, not a tool used on a running one: it is the only process
# that applies the schema and the only one that puts jars in volumes, so without a run of `apply`
# there is no bot and no server. Its database credentials come from the same NORDTAL_DATABASE_* /
# POSTGRES_* values as everything else and need no lines of their own.
#
# Which release it FOLLOWS. There is no second variable describing what the servers run any more -
# what a server runs is the jar lying in its volume, and this is the only line that decides where a
# new one would come from. 'latest' asks GitHub's /releases/latest, which skips drafts and
# pre-releases - so a release left unpublished is invisible here, and "the update did not arrive"
# looks the same as "nobody pressed Publish". An exact tag pins it, and that is how a rollback is
# expressed.
UPDATER_SEASON_RELEASE=latest
UPDATER_DISPLAY_TAGS_RELEASE=latest
#
# THERE IS NO PLATFORM VERSION AND NO BUILD PIN IN THIS FILE, and both are deliberate. The Minecraft
# version and Velocity's major are eu.nordtal.s2.common.Platform, compiled into every one of our
# jars, because a platform version is a property of the season rather than of an installation. There
# is no build pin either: what a server runs is the newest STABLE build, resolved by the updater and
# by the container's own entrypoint.
#
# Whether `updater serve` fills EMPTY volumes before it reports itself ready. Leave it on.
#
# This is what makes a deployment possible with nobody typing anything on the host: a Minecraft
# server refuses to start on an empty plugins folder, and Arcane has no way to run
# `docker compose run --rm updater bootstrap`. With this on, the first start of the updater
# container downloads what is missing and only then writes the readiness marker.
#
# IT CANNOT MOVE A VERSION. Only artefacts with NOTHING installed are fetched; anything that
# already has a jar keeps it, however old. So a restart of a running network finds nothing missing
# and does nothing at all, and upgrades stay a request somebody makes from Discord or in game.
#
# Turning it off means the servers refuse to start until an apply has run, and say so by name.
#UPDATER_BOOTSTRAP=true
#
# Optional. GitHub allows 60 unauthenticated requests per hour per IP and a run makes two, so this
# only matters on a host that shares its address with something busier. Public read is enough -
# the updater never writes to GitHub.
#UPDATER_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The restart, through Arcane
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEAVE ARCANE_URL EMPTY AND NOTHING BREAKS. Every other part of the updater works; the restart
# button in Discord and `/smp update restart` in game both answer "Arcane is not configured" and
# tell you to click Redeploy in Arcane yourself. That is a supported state, not a broken one.
#
# NOT the Docker socket, deliberately: a container holding /var/run/docker.sock can do anything on
# the host, and this is the container whose whole job is downloading files from the internet and
# putting them where servers will execute them. One API token is the cheaper half of that trade,
# and the redeploy then shows up in Arcane's own history.
#
# ARCANE_URL          the origin, no trailing slash: https://arcane.example.com
#                     HTTPS, in production, and that is not a preference: ARCANE_API_KEY travels on
#                     every redeploy as an X-Api-Key header, and on http:// it travels in the clear
#                     to anything that can see the connection - which is a key to redeploy every
#                     project in that Arcane. Plain HTTP is warned about at startup and is only
#                     defensible on the local stack, where the request never leaves the machine.
#                     NOT http://localhost:<port>, however right it looks in your browser bar.
#                     The updater is a CONTAINER; localhost inside it is that container, and the
#                     restart fails with a connection error in milliseconds while Arcane runs
#                     perfectly. Use http://host.docker.internal:<port> - compose.yml maps that
#                     name for this service - or Arcane's container name if it shares a network
#                     with the stack, or the host's address. A loopback value is warned about on
#                     startup and named again in the failure written into update_request.result.
# ARCANE_API_KEY      generated in Arcane under Settings -> API Keys. Sent as X-Api-Key; the
#                     permission the redeploy needs is `projects:deploy`.
# ARCANE_ENVIRONMENT  which Docker environment, AS ITS ID. Arcane's own host is 0; a remote agent
#                     is a UUID. Leave it alone unless Arcane manages this host through an agent.
# ARCANE_PROJECT      which project, AS ITS ID - a UUID Arcane generated. THIS IS NOT THE COMPOSE
#                     PROJECT NAME: `nordtal-s2` here answers 404. Read it out of the browser URL
#                     with the project open, or from
#                       curl -H "X-Api-Key: $ARCANE_API_KEY" $ARCANE_URL/api/environments/0/projects
#                     There is no default and the updater refuses to start without it once
#                     ARCANE_URL is set - an ID is not something anyone can guess.
#
# ARCANE_RUNTIME_PATH and ARCANE_CONTAINER_PATH are the two a run actually uses, and neither needs
# a person:
# GET  /api/environments/{id}/projects/{projectId}/runtime  - every service with its container id,
#      its status and its Docker health, which is what turns "and then everything came back" from
#      a hope into a check.
# POST /api/environments/{id}/containers/{containerId}/{start|stop} - one container at a time,
#      which is the only shape that leaves a GAP between stopping and starting. The jars are
#      replaced in that gap; Arcane's project-level calls do both in one request and leave none.
#      It is also why the updater survives its own update: it never stops itself.
#
# ARCANE_REDEPLOY_PATH is not on any production path - the project-wide redeploy took this container
# down with everything else, so nothing was left to report whether the network returned - but the
# code still has the call and an operator may want it by hand. It stays a setting because Arcane's
# documentation does not publish the path, so a version that moves it is one line here rather than a
# release of ours. Leave it commented to use the default.
#
# WATCH THE FIRST PRESS. getarcaneapp/arcane#1943 reports a redeploy of an ALREADY RUNNING project
# doing nothing while still answering success - which is exactly this case, since the stack is up
# when the button is pressed. Nothing in the updater can detect it; watch the containers cycle.
#ARCANE_URL=
#ARCANE_API_KEY=
#ARCANE_ENVIRONMENT=0
#ARCANE_PROJECT=
#ARCANE_REDEPLOY_PATH=/api/environments/{environment}/projects/{project}/redeploy
#ARCANE_RUNTIME_PATH=/api/environments/{environment}/projects/{project}/runtime
#ARCANE_CONTAINER_PATH=/api/environments/{environment}/containers/{container}/{action}
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THIS FILE LIVES WHEN ARCANE RUNS THE DEPLOYMENT
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not on the host's filesystem, and not in this repository. Arcane keeps three files for a git
# synced project: `.env.git` from the repository, `project.env` for what you type into its
# interface, and the effective `.env` it writes from both. A sync never overwrites your values.
#
# Since .env is gitignored here, `.env.git` stays empty and EVERYTHING BELOW IS TYPED INTO ARCANE.
# This file is the reference for what to type, not something to copy onto a server.
#
# The project settings that go with it, once:
#
#   Auto Sync            on - Arcane polls this repository on syncInterval and picks up compose.yml
#   Redeploy After Sync  OFF - a redeploy takes the four Minecraft servers down for minutes, and a
#                        commit must not do that to people who are playing. The updater's own
#                        restart button exists for that and gives players a countdown first.
#   Pull policy          always pull latest - IMAGE_TAG=latest means nothing else would ever fetch
#                        a new image
#   Project root         the REPOSITORY root, not deploy/ - compose.yml is up there for this reason

# Server software: NOTHING TO SET, and no PAPER_VERSION, PAPER_BUILD, VELOCITY_VERSION or
# VELOCITY_BUILD belongs here.
#
# The version is a literal in compose.yml, taken from eu.nordtal.s2.common.Platform and asserted
# against it by :updater's TopologyTest. The build is resolved: the updater installs the newest
# STABLE build into each server's .server/ cache, and when that cache is empty the entrypoint asks
# the Fill API for the same thing.

# ==============================================================================================
# Images
# ==============================================================================================
# ALL FOUR NORDTAL IMAGES ARE PULLED FROM ghcr.io/nordtal AND NONE IS BUILT ON THE HOST.
# .github/workflows/release.yml pushes minecraft, updater, postgres-backup and discord-bot when a
# release is published, each under the release version and under `latest`.
#
# THIS IS NOT COSMETIC. Arcane deploys by PULLING - its Redeploy never builds - so an image that
# exists only in a host's local Docker makes a deploy fail with:
#
#   failed to pull image ghcr.io/nordtal/minecraft:local: ... error from registry: denied
#
# `denied` is also what a PRIVATE package answers, which is the second half of the same trap: a
# package under an organisation is private on its first push. All four have to be set to Public in
# their GitHub package settings, once, or Arcane needs a registry credential instead.
#
# IMAGE_TAG picks which release runs. `latest` is the default; pinning it is the rollback, and it
# is one line rather than four:
#
#   IMAGE_TAG=0.2.1
#
IMAGE_TAG=latest

# One image somewhere else entirely - a locally built one, or a fork. These win over IMAGE_TAG and
# exist for the case it does not cover.
#MC_IMAGE=ghcr.io/nordtal/minecraft:0.2.1
#BOT_IMAGE=
#UPDATER_IMAGE=
#BACKUP_IMAGE=

# The PostgreSQL image. 17-alpine is the version every integration test in this repository runs
# against; changing the major version is a database migration, not a config edit.
#POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:17-alpine

# ==============================================================================================
# Minecraft
# ==============================================================================================
# Accepting https://aka.ms/MinecraftEULA. Deliberately not defaulted anywhere in the image.
EULA=true

# Velocity modern forwarding. Generate once:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 24
#
# It reaches all four services from here and nothing has to be pasted into a config file: the
# proxy container writes it to /data/forwarding.secret, and each Paper backend gets it as
# PAPER_VELOCITY_SECRET, which Paper reads from the environment on purpose (PaperMC/Paper#10127).
#
# Paper does then write it into its own config/paper-global.yml, so the value does end up in the
# three backend volumes - rotating it means changing this line AND that one, per backend.
#
# A mismatch - which now means "one of the four containers did not get this variable" - refuses
# every login with "Unable to connect you to the backend server".
VELOCITY_FORWARDING_SECRET=REPLACE_ME

PROXY_BIND=0.0.0.0
PROXY_PORT=25565

# SIMPLE VOICE CHAT HAS NO VARIABLE HERE, and that is the whole point of installing its Velocity
# plugin on the proxy. Voice audio is UDP; with the proxy plugin the network needs exactly one open
# UDP port, and it is PROXY_PORT's number on PROXY_BIND's address - compose publishes 25565/udp
# beside 25565/tcp, and the plugin works out which backend each player is on by itself.
#
# So: open UDP 25565 in the host firewall alongside TCP, and nothing else. No per-backend port, no
# per-backend voice_host, no editing voicechat-server.properties by hand. Setting PROXY_PORT to
# something other than 25565 moves only the Minecraft port; see the note in compose.yml.

# Heaps. Unmeasured starting points, not measurements - the SMP's is the one that matters and it
# should be set from the real host once Nordtal's pre-generation has been run.
PROXY_HEAP=1G
LIMBO_HEAP=1G
# HOW MANY PLAYERS THE NETWORK ACCEPTS, and the only player number in the whole deployment - there
# is nothing else to keep it in step with.
#
# The proxy enforces it at the login gate - where it can say why - and advertises the same number in
# the server browser. All three Paper backends are set to it as well, so a tab list shows the number
# the browser promised instead of one only the servers know about. Admins are exempt, so a full
# network can still be entered by whoever has to go and fix it; the backends let those same admins
# past their own cap, which is what keeps one number from costing anything.
#
# CHANGING IT NEEDS THE BACKENDS RESTARTED, not just the proxy: each Paper container writes its
# server.properties on start. `docker compose restart network-control limbo hunger-games smp`.
#
# There is no BACKEND_MAX_PLAYERS: a backend enforcing its own limit means "Server full" arrives
# AFTER a successful login, a resource-pack download and a wait in the waiting room. Delete it from
# your .env if it is there; it does nothing.
#NETWORK_MAX_PLAYERS=500

# WHAT THE SERVER BROWSER SHOWS, one MOTD per season phase.
#
# MiniMessage, so <gradient>, <#rrggbb> and <newline> all work - <newline> is how you get the second
# line every client shows. Placeholders are written in braces and are listed in network.yml, which
# is where these live and where the shipped defaults are; anything left unset here keeps the default
# from that file, and those defaults are real MOTDs rather than placeholders.
#
# These are plugin config values read at proxy start, not something seeded into velocity.toml once -
# a MOTD on a fresh volume only is a MOTD nobody can change afterwards.
#
# THE NAME IS ALWAYS #4a63d8 AND NOTHING ELSE - the logo's own blue, lightened for a list the client
# paints almost black. BrandColourTest fails a phase that colours the name for itself again; the
# phase is what the second line is for.
#
# THERE ARE NO GLYPHS HERE and there cannot be: the server browser draws the MOTD in the client's
# own font, before any resource pack has been fetched. A private-use code point renders as a box.
#
#NETWORK_MOTD_PRE_LAUNCH=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Season 2 opens in <white>{countdown}</white></gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_PRE_EVENT=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Hunger Games: <white>{hg-participants}</white> players in <white>{hg-teams}</white> teams</gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_START_EVENT=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Hunger Games: <white>{hg-alive}</white> of <white>{hg-participants}</white> still alive</gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_SMP=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Season 2 running - <white>{smp-milestones-done}</white> of <white>{smp-milestones-total}</white> milestones done</gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_MAINTENANCE=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Maintenance - back shortly, the season is not over</gray>

HUNGER_GAMES_HEAP=4G
SMP_HEAP=6G

# The whole JVM command line for every Minecraft service, if the heap settings above are not
# enough. Replaces them completely, so a value here has to carry -Xms/-Xmx itself.
#JVM_OPTS=

# Which backends the proxy knows, and where a player lands. Server names must match
# network-control's gate.yml#server-* keys; hostnames are compose service names. Only used when
# velocity.toml does not exist yet - the file is seeded once and then belongs to the operator.
#VELOCITY_SERVERS=limbo=limbo:25565 hunger-games=hunger-games:25565 smp=smp:25565
#VELOCITY_TRY=limbo

# There is no SMP_EXTRA_PLUGIN_URLS: three URLs with three versions in them, maintained by hand in
# a file nobody opens between deployments, is what the updater exists to end. It resolves DisplayTags
# from its own repository's releases and PacketEvents and Chunky from Modrinth, filtered to this
# Minecraft version and `paper`.
#
# They are still REQUIRED and nothing about that changed: smp's paper-plugin.yml declares
# DisplayTags load: BEFORE, required: true, and PacketEvents is required under it. The guard moved
# into entrypoint.sh, which refuses to start on an empty plugins folder.

# The world-generation datapacks, and the world Paper reads them from.
#
# Datapacks are SERVER-GLOBAL and are read only from <level-name>/datapacks/; there is no per-world
# datapack API. So this one folder feeds Nordtal, the farm world, the Nether and the End alike, and
# the farm world's nightly regeneration inherits the packs with nothing copied.
#
# They are fetched BEFORE the server starts, because worldgen registries are read once: a pack
# dropped in afterwards changes no terrain, and terrain is never re-rolled once it is on disk.
#
# Format: whitespace-separated <sha512>@<url> entries (a bare URL works and only warns). sha512
# rather than sha256, because sha512 is what Modrinth publishes and a pin nobody can re-derive is a
# pin that rots.
#
# LEVEL_NAME must agree with server.properties#level-name AND with the smp plugin's
# config.yml#world-nordtal. It is the world that holds the built spawn, the world the datapacks
# live in, and the world every other one is created inside.
#
# The entrypoint WRITES it into server.properties on a volume that has none. On a volume that
# already has a level-name saying something else the container REFUSES TO START: renaming does not
# move a world, it generates an empty one beside it. The message names both values and the two ways
# out.
#
# ONE EXCEPTION: a volume still carrying Paper's own default `world` is repaired rather than
# refused, provided nobody has ever logged out in it - that is not a name anybody chose, it is what
# Paper writes when nothing tells it otherwise. `world` and its two dimension folders are deleted
# and the name corrected. Any OTHER disagreement is still fatal.
# See deploy/README.md#first-start-seeding.
#SMP_DATAPACK_URLS=f5283548323149ecd3f218b8efbe03c1c582df3f40b6bb3b095ba9e4cac0c87108b98adabd6ebd3a8037ec239773a253f75b8bee7db52e6f5663bf9dc63c2ea3@https://cdn.modrinth.com/data/8oi3bsk5/versions/CzijfXJQ/Terralith_26.2_v2.6.4.zip cf026b67f4fc0f5ab15229995f11faf43fdd41b29777a6756151276e83da65f887fe03b6356c5011f8ba32371f98941778928e4258e81b4c97411a40af1635ca@https://cdn.modrinth.com/data/tpehi7ww/versions/CS77UwHE/Dungeons%20and%20Taverns%20v5.3.2.zip
#SMP_LEVEL_NAME=nordtal

# The seed Nordtal is generated from. It is pre-generated ONCE, to its final border of 4000, and
# then frozen for the whole season - so this is the one number here that cannot be corrected later
# by editing a file. The entrypoint writes it only while the world does not exist yet; against a
# volume that already holds one it compares and warns, and never re-rolls anything. "Does not exist
# yet" is <level-name>/level.dat and NOT the folder: the folder is created by the datapack fetch
# above before Paper generates anything.
#
# Terralith and Dungeons and Taverns decide what a seed actually produces, so this number describes
# the same landscape only with SMP_DATAPACK_URLS above unchanged. The hunger games world is
# hand-built and shipped as a folder, so its own level-seed is never consulted.
#SMP_LEVEL_SEED=1837371427

# The hand-built hunger games map. The plugin looks for HungerGamesSpec#world-name and disables
# itself when that world is not loaded, so this is what brings it up - and it is the folder
# deploy/README.md#getting-a-world-into-a-volume copies into.
#HUNGER_GAMES_LEVEL_NAME=hunger_games

# Where each server's plugins/ lives. LEAVE ALL FOUR UNSET ON A PRODUCTION HOST - the defaults are
# named volumes (mc-<service>-plugins), which is the whole point of them.
#
# They exist because plugins/ is the only part of a server that a person edits: every plugin's
# config.yml, smp's milestones.yml and sounds.yml, and the proxy's pack.yml. Keeping it out of the
# world volume means Arcane's backup policy can cover the configs without copying four gigabytes of
# terrain, and it means a jar swap does not touch the world.
#
# NEVER POINT ONE INTO THE CHECKOUT: Arcane's GitOps sync deletes ignored files from the directory
# it pulls, so the first sync after a hand edit takes every deployed config with it and every server
# comes back writing fresh defaults, with nothing saying so.
#
# A value with a `/` in it is a bind mount to Docker; a value without one is a volume name. That is
# the only difference, so setting one of these to a path is deliberate and is what the LOCAL stack
# does - see deploy/dev.env.example. The updater mounts the very same four expressions; if these
# are ever set by hand, both sides move together or the updater installs where no server reads.
#NETWORK_CONTROL_PLUGINS=mc-network-control-plugins
#LIMBO_PLUGINS=mc-limbo-plugins
#HUNGER_GAMES_PLUGINS=mc-hunger-games-plugins
#SMP_PLUGINS=mc-smp-plugins

# When the SMP asks the updater for the network's nightly volume backup, HH:mm. Empty means never,
# and then nothing in the network asks for one at all - the updater deliberately does not schedule
# its own work, so this is the only clock there is.
#
# The run it asks for is the same one /backup now asks for: thirty seconds of countdown every
# player sees, smp/network-control/bot stopped, one Arcane snapshot per volume in
# updater.yml#backup.volumes, everything started again and watched until it reports healthy.
#
# It needs Arcane configured - see ARCANE_URL above. Without it every night's run fails at the
# first step having stopped nothing.
# Setting it to an empty value really does turn it off: compose interpolates this one with a single
# dash (${SMP_BACKUP_TIME-04:45}), so an empty entry is passed through as empty rather than falling
# back. Every other setting in this file uses `:-` and treats empty as unset.
#SMP_BACKUP_TIME=04:45

# ==============================================================================================
# PostgreSQL
# ==============================================================================================
# One database, five clients: the bot (which is the only one that migrates) and the four
# Minecraft services. They all get these credentials through compose.yml - there is nothing to
# fill in per plugin.
POSTGRES_DB=nordtal
POSTGRES_USER=nordtal
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=REPLACE_ME
# Loopback only. Everything that reads this database is in the same stack; the publish exists for
# a psql from the host. Backups do NOT go through it - the sidecar below reaches the database over
# the compose network.
POSTGRES_BIND=127.0.0.1
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# ==============================================================================================
# Database backup (profile `backup`)
# ==============================================================================================
# A pg_dump into the `postgres-dumps` volume, daily. Point Arcane's volume backup policy at THAT
# volume and not at `postgres-data`: Arcane stops the containers using a volume only when the
# policy's `Stop Containers` flag is set, and for a live PGDATA both settings are wrong - see
# README.md#backups. Everything here has a working default; nothing needs filling in.
#
# Time of day in TZ above, 24-hour. A value that is not HH:MM stops the container rather than
# quietly becoming "never".
#BACKUP_AT=04:00
# How many dumps to keep in the volume. Offsite retention is Arcane's job, not this one's.
#BACKUP_KEEP=14
# Dump once at start-up as well. Leave it on: it turns a wrong password into an error you see
# while deploying instead of a gap you find at 04:00 tomorrow.
#BACKUP_RUN_ON_START=true
# Back up a database this compose does not run. Defaults are the POSTGRES_* values above.
#BACKUP_PGHOST=db.example.org
#BACKUP_PGPORT=5432
#BACKUP_PGDATABASE=nordtal
#BACKUP_PGUSER=nordtal
#BACKUP_PGPASSWORD=

# Point the WHOLE stack at a database outside it: set these three, then drop `db` from
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. The hostname `postgres` in the default URL is this compose's own service.
# These three reach the bot and all four Minecraft services - compose.yml hands each process the
# same values under the prefix it reads (NORDTAL_SMP_DATABASE_*, and so on). There is no
# per-service override; if one ever needs different credentials, that is an edit to compose.yml.
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_JDBC_URL=jdbc:postgresql://db.example.org:5432/nordtal
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_USERNAME=nordtal
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_PASSWORD=

# The bot's pool and SQL logging. Only the bot reads these two.
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE=10
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_LOG_SQL=false

# ==============================================================================================
# Discord bot - credentials
# ==============================================================================================
# https://discord.com/developers/applications -> your application -> Bot -> Reset Token.
NORDTAL_BOT_TOKEN=REPLACE_ME

# bunq: the API key and the id of the monetary account that is polled and billed. The account id
# is a number, not a snowflake and not an IBAN.
NORDTAL_BOT_BUNQ_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME
NORDTAL_BOT_BUNQ_ACCOUNT_ID=REPLACE_ME
# ==============================================================================================
# Discord bot - the guild
# ==============================================================================================
# The one guild the bot manages. Every id below is a role or channel inside it.
NORDTAL_ACCESS_GUILD_ID=REPLACE_ME

# --- roles ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# access      strictly bot-owned: added and removed to match the database, so granting it by hand
#             holds only until the next reconcile. /grant-access is the way.
# donor       granted on a donation and never taken away, by anything. Safe to hand out manually.
# admin       read-only for the bot: mirrored into discord_user.admin, and that flag is what
#             authorises /phase set and the MAINTENANCE phase everywhere else. Not cosmetic.
# admin-ping  who gets mentioned in the admin channel. Grants no power; may be the same role.
NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_ACCESS=REPLACE_ME
NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_DONOR=REPLACE_ME
NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_ADMIN=REPLACE_ME
NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_ADMIN_PING=REPLACE_ME

# --- channels ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The only channel that is not per-language: everything a human may need to act on - unmatchable
# payments, payments on an expired reference, failed DMs, role errors, every link and unlink.
NORDTAL_ACCESS_CHANNELS_ADMIN=REPLACE_ME

# ==============================================================================================
# Discord bot - languages
# ==============================================================================================
# Every language the network speaks, and the four Discord objects each one needs. This is the
# ONLY place they are configured; there are no per-language settings anywhere else.
#
#   tag                     lower case, the name of the .properties bundle in every module's
#                           messages/ directory, and the value stored in discord_user.locale.
#                           'en' is mandatory - it is what a missing translation falls back to.
#   role                    the role Discord's own onboarding assigns for this language.
#                           Read-only for the bot; no role at all means English.
#   contribution-channel    carries the buy-access message and its donation thank-yous.
#   link-channel            carries the account-link message.
#   hunger-games-channel    carries the Register message. A separate channel on purpose:
#                           registering for the start event is not buying access, and access is
#                           not required to play.
#
# A third language is an edit here plus <tag>.properties in every module - not a release. The
# order matters in one place only: it is the order the link-code disconnect screen prints.
#
# JSON, because this is a list of structures and .env has no other way to carry one. The quotes
# and the line breaks are both fine - docker compose reads a single-quoted multi-line value as one
# string.
#
# "status-channel" is the one OPTIONAL id in the block and is empty here on purpose. Fill it in
# with a channel whose NAME the bot may rewrite - a voice channel is the usual shape, because
# Discord keeps a voice channel's name verbatim and lower-cases a text channel's - and the sidebar
# then carries the countdown to the opening and, after it, what the network is doing. Left empty,
# that language simply has no status channel and the bot renames nothing.
NORDTAL_ACCESS_LANGUAGES='[
  {
    "tag": "en",
    "role": "REPLACE_ME",
    "contribution-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "link-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "hunger-games-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "status-channel": ""
  },
  {
    "tag": "de",
    "role": "REPLACE_ME",
    "contribution-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "link-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "hunger-games-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "status-channel": ""
  }
]'

# ==============================================================================================
# Discord bot - the product
# ==============================================================================================
# What can be bought. Leave this unset to use the agreed price list, which is what a fresh
# access.yml is written with: 30 days for 3 EUR, 60 for 5, 90 for 7.
#
# Set it to change the offer without a release. Rules, all checked at startup: at least one
# entry, day counts unique, and more days must cost more. A tier is identified by its day count -
# that is what a purchase button carries - so changing `days` on an existing entry retires that
# tier rather than repricing it.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_TIERS='[
#  {"days": 30, "price-cents": 300},
#  {"days": 60, "price-cents": 500},
#  {"days": 90, "price-cents": 700}
#]'

# The optional surcharge that grants the permanent donor role, in cents. Also the threshold at
# which money left over above an order is read as a donation rather than ignored.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_DONATION_CENTS=500

# ==============================================================================================
# Discord bot - timings
# ==============================================================================================
# All optional; empty means "use what is in access.yml", which is what these values are.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_REQUEST_TTL_HOURS=24
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_RECENT_PAYMENT_COUNT=50
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_EXPIRY_REMINDER_LEAD_DAYS=3
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLE_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=10

# LEAVE THIS EMPTY ON A FRESH DATABASE. The first start stamps its own instant into bot_setting
# and no later start rewrites it. Without a cut-off the first poll books up to 50 historical bunq
# payments - grants, roles, DMs and public thank-yous included.
#
# Set it only to choose a different cut-off deliberately: ISO-8601, UTC, e.g.
# 2026-09-01T00:00:00Z. A value here overrides the stored one without replacing it, so emptying
# this again falls back to the original first-start instant.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_WATERMARK=
